? The "naive" assumption in naive Bayes is:
+ Attributes are conditionally independent of each other given the class
- All classes are equally likely
- Attributes are numeric
- The data has no missing values
= This makes P(X|C) the product of individual P(xₖ|C).

? Bayes' theorem is:
+ P(H|X) = P(X|H) · P(H) ÷ P(X)
- P(H|X) = P(H) ÷ P(X|H)
- P(X|H) = P(H|X) · P(X)
- P(H|X) = P(X) · P(H)
= P(H|X) is the posterior; P(H) is the prior.

? P(S|M) = 0.5, P(M) = 1/50,000, P(S) = 1/20. What is P(M|S)?
# 0.0002 ~0.00001
= 0.5 × (1/50,000) ÷ (1/20) = 0.0002.

? To classify X, naive Bayes chooses the class that maximises:
+ P(X|Cᵢ) · P(Cᵢ)
- P(X)
- P(Cᵢ) only
- P(X|Cᵢ) ÷ P(Cᵢ)
= P(X) is the same for every class, so it can be ignored.

? Of 9 "yes" tuples, 2 have age ≤ 30. What is P(age ≤ 30 | yes) (3 decimals)?
# 0.222 ~0.001
= 2 ÷ 9 = 0.222.

? For a continuous attribute such as age, naive Bayes usually estimates P(xₖ|Cᵢ) with:
+ A Gaussian (normal) density using the class mean and standard deviation
- A simple count
- The median
- A decision tree
= g(x, μ, σ) = 1/(√(2π)σ) · e^(−(x−μ)²/2σ²).

? A zero count (e.g. no "no" tuple with income = high) makes the whole product zero. The fix is:
+ The Laplacian correction (add 1 to each count)
- Delete the attribute
- Multiply by 100
- Use gain ratio
= The corrected estimates stay close to the originals.

? Final scores: yes = 0.044 × 0.643 = 0.028; no = 0.019 × 0.357 = 0.007. The predicted class is:
+ yes
- no
- Can't decide
- Both
= The larger P(X|C)·P(C) wins.

? Rule R1 covers 2 of 14 tuples and classifies both correctly. What is its coverage (%)?
# 14.29 ~0.05
= 2 ÷ 14 = 14.29%; accuracy = 2 ÷ 2 = 100%.

? Rules extracted from a decision tree (one per root-to-leaf path) are:
+ Mutually exclusive and exhaustive
- Always overlapping
- Unordered and incomplete
- Always fewer than the leaves
= Each tuple is covered by exactly one rule.

? Bayesian belief networks improve on naive Bayes by:
+ Allowing dependencies between attributes, shown in a graph
- Removing probabilities
- Using only one attribute
- Needing no training data
= They model conditional dependencies with a directed acyclic graph and probability tables.
